  "notes": "Danish electropop vocalist and the defining topline voice of the moombahton-pop crossover. The band spans the whole 98-128 the entry gives, but the mode is set at the documented 108 because that is the number the research states outright — \"the moombahton records sit at around 108 BPM (documented for the idiom)\" — and it is the grammar her drums line describes: \"a slowed electro-house kick with a dembow/tresillo snare accent rather than a plain backbeat, plus 16th shaker\". So the kick leaves the four-square grid for an anchor on 1 with a 0.38 tresillo bias, the snare keeps a 2&4 placement but carries the dembow \"and-a\" figure as a high-probability ghost set, and the perc bed is shaker and conga. The low end is \"808-adjacent sub with short glides on the moombahton records; filtered pumping saw bass on the house records\", so `bass808` is `counter_riff` and the saw bass survives as the bassline. The structural device is the entry's: \"the vocal chorus is often replaced by an instrumental one built from her chopped voice\", which is why the melody carries a chopped-vocal timbre and the counter is a bell echo. Her delivery is \"mid-register, nasal, slightly strained… written in a narrow band\". ⚠ The research marks the key and delivery reading `[LOW-CONF]` and calls the other parameters lane-derived; both marks are repeated here, and every velocity and density is INFERRED.",
